; Control characters occupy no columns when Ledger measures text.
;
; mk_wcwidth() gives C0 and C1 control characters and DEL a width of -1;
; unistring::width() counts them as zero columns instead of adding -1
; into a std::size_t.  The first payee is "Pay", U+0081 (a C1 control)
; and "ee": five columns.  The first account is "Assets:X", two SOH
; (0x01) bytes and "Y": nine columns, which a 12-column field pads with
; three spaces and an 8-column field abbreviates by one column.  The
; second account is four SOH bytes and "X:A": three columns; summing -1
; per SOH wrapped its width around below zero, and reg aborted while
; abbreviating it.  These characters are invisible in most editors.
;
; The C1 control is U+0081 and not NEL (U+0085), FS, GS or RS: Python's
; str.splitlines() ends a line at those, and the semantic harnesses
; (test/semantic/check_all.py, footing.py) read this journal with it.

2024/01/01 Payee
    Assets:XY    10 EUR
    Equity

2024/01/02 Opening
    X:A    10 EUR
    Equity

test reg --format '[%-12(account)]\n' Assets
[Assets:XY   ]
end test

test reg --format '|%-8(payee)|\n' Assets
|Payee   |
end test

test reg --account-width 8 Assets
24-Jan-01 Payee                 Asset:XY       10 EUR       10 EUR
end test

test reg
24-Jan-01 Payee                 Assets:XY                    10 EUR       10 EUR
                                Equity                      -10 EUR            0
24-Jan-02 Opening               X:A                          10 EUR       10 EUR
                                Equity                      -10 EUR            0
end test
